==============================================================================
0. CIRCLE-GEOMETRY CORE  (this is what decoupling rests on, exactly)
==============================================================================
For centre y with |y| = rho and radius r, every sample point z = y + r u
satisfies  |z - 0| >= r - rho  and  |z - 0| <= r + rho.  Hence if
  rho < eps/2  and  r > 1 - eps/2   then   |z| > 1 - eps  (strictly).
  eps=1/10     rho=1/40     r=0.975000  r-rho=0.950000  > R=1-eps=0.900000 ? True
  eps=1/100    rho=1/400    r=0.997500  r-rho=0.995000  > R=1-eps=0.990000 ? True
  eps=1/1000   rho=1/4000   r=0.999750  r-rho=0.999500  > R=1-eps=0.999000 ? True

==============================================================================
1. EXAMPLE 1 (R = 1, i.e. eps = 0): c1 = 1_{|z|>=1},  c0' = 1 everywhere
   c1 = c0' on {|z| >= 1};  they differ on the OPEN unit disc.
   colour-0 angular fraction on the circle of radius r about the origin:
==============================================================================
                 r    frac(colour 0) for c1    brute force      err
        0.50000000               1.00000000     1.00000000  0.0e+00
        0.90000000               1.00000000     1.00000000  0.0e+00
        0.99000000               1.00000000     1.00000000  0.0e+00
        0.99990000               1.00000000     1.00000000  0.0e+00
        0.99999900               1.00000000     1.00000000  0.0e+00
        1.00000000               0.00000000     0.00000000  0.0e+00
        1.00000100               0.00000000     0.00000000  0.0e+00
        1.01000000               0.00000000     0.00000000  0.0e+00
  => colour 0 has FULL angular measure on every circle of radius r < 1
     about the origin, colour 1 full measure for r > 1.  Both colours are
     therefore palette colours at 0 for c1 (witnesses y_n=0, r_n=1-1/n
     and y_n=0, r_n=1+1/n), while c0' = constant 1 has palette {1} alone.

==============================================================================
