3. EXAMPLE 3 (thin annulus, delta = 1/100): cA = 0 on 1-delta<|z|<1,
   c0' = 1 everywhere.  These agree on {|z| >= 1} AND on {|z| <= 1-delta},
   i.e. they differ only in the annulus (1/100 away from the sphere),
   well inside the open unit ball.
==============================================================================
       rho            r    frac inside |z|<1   frac outside |z|>1-d    => frac(colour 0)
  0.000000     0.999000             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.000000     0.995000             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.000000     0.995000             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.000000     0.999999             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.000000     1.000000             0.000000               0.000000             0.000000
  0.000000     1.001000             0.000000               0.000000             0.000000
  0.001000     0.999000             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.001000     0.995000             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.001000     0.995000             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.001000     0.999999             0.500159               0.000000             0.500159
  0.001000     1.000000             0.499841               0.000000             0.499841
  0.001000     1.001000             0.000000               0.000000             0.000000
  0.005000     0.999000             0.563314               0.000000             0.563314
  0.005000     0.995000             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.005000     0.995000             1.000000               0.000000             1.000000
  0.005000     0.999999             0.499268               0.000000             0.499268
  0.005000     1.000000             0.499204               0.000000             0.499204
  0.005000     1.001000             0.435127               0.000000             0.435127
  => colour 0 has full angular measure on every circle with
     1-delta < r < 1 and rho small: witness (y_n=0, r_n=1-1/n) for n large.
     colour 1 likewise (r_n = 1+1/n).  So P^cA_0 = {0,1} a.e. vs
     P^c0'_0 = {1} a.e.: the palettes differ although the two colourings
     agree off B(0,1) AND on the whole ball B(0,1-1/100).

==============================================================================
4. CONTROL (the positive side, eps > 0).  Pair: cb = 0 on {|z| < 1-eps},
   1 elsewhere, and c0' = 1 everywhere.  They agree EXACTLY on
   {|z| >= 1-eps}, so the theorem applies with this eps.  In the
   decoupling window rho < eps/2, r > 1-eps/2 we have min|z| = r-rho > 
   1-eps, so no circle meets the disagreement region: frac0 = 0 for the
   bad colouring too, and both window-palettes are {1}.  (eps = 1/100.)
==============================================================================
   rho < eps/2    r > 1-eps/2       min|z|    > 1-eps    frac0(cb)    frac0(c0')
      0.000000       0.995001     0.995001       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.000000       0.995000     0.995000       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.000000       1.000000     1.000000       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.000000       1.020000     1.020000       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.002500       0.995001     0.992501       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.002500       0.995000     0.992500       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.002500       1.000000     0.997500       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.002500       1.020000     1.017500       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.004999       0.995001     0.990002       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.004999       0.995000     0.990001       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.004999       1.000000     0.995001       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
      0.004999       1.020000     1.015001       True 0.000000e+00      0.000000
  Exactly zero (the closed form returns the constant branch: the circle
  of radius r about a point at distance rho with r-1 > -eps/2, rho < eps/2
  has min|z| = r-rho > 1-eps, i.e. kappa <= -1 when 1-eps <= min|z|),
  so the palettes of cb and c0' agree, as the theorem predicts.
==============================================================================
5. SAMPLER CROSS-CHECK of the closed-form arc lengths (should agree to
   O(1/N) = 1.91e-06):
==============================================================================
  rho= 0.300 r= 1.000 a= 1.000  closed=0.45207263 sampled=0.45207405  |diff|=1.42e-06
  rho= 0.300 r= 0.900 a= 0.990  closed=0.54739085 sampled=0.54739189  |diff|=1.04e-06
  rho= 0.001 r= 0.999 a= 1.000  closed=0.99999992 sampled=0.99999809  |diff|=1.83e-06
  rho= 0.500 r= 1.200 a= 1.000  closed=0.30500204 sampled=0.30500221  |diff|=1.69e-07

Summary: the palette at 0 of the radius-1 trace is NOT determined by c
off the OPEN ball B(0,1): examples 1 and 3 both violate it, and they
violate it on a set of directions of FULL angular measure.
